TYPES OF WELDING

SMAW
Shielded Metal Arc Welding (Stick Welding)
Heat source: electric arc
Electrode: consumable flux-coated stick
Shielding: flux coating + slag
Key variables: amperage, electrode diameter, polarity, travel speed, arc length
Fundamentals: portable, works outdoors, all-position, slag must be chipped

GMAW (MIG/MAG)
Gas Metal Arc Welding
Heat source: electric arc
Electrode: continuously fed solid wire
Shielding: external gas (inert = MIG, active = MAG)
Key variables: wire feed speed, voltage, gas type/flow, stick-out
Fundamentals: high deposition, clean welds, less portable than stick

GTAW (TIG)
Gas Tungsten Arc Welding
Heat source: electric arc
Electrode: non-consumable tungsten
Shielding: inert gas (usually argon)
Filler: separate rod added manually
Key variables: current (AC/DC), amperage, gas flow, tungsten type/grind
Fundamentals: precise, high-quality, thin metals, slower process

RESISTANCE SPOT WELDING
Heat source: electrical resistance at contact point
Electrodes: copper alloy tips that clamp sheets
Shielding: none
Key variables: current, weld time, electrode force, hold time
Fundamentals: high-speed sheet joining, common in auto bodywork

OXY-FUEL WELDING
(e.g. Oxy-Acetylene)
Heat source: combustion flame
Electrode: none (filler rod optional)
Shielding: flame envelope
Key variables: oxygen-to-fuel ratio, tip size, flame type (neutral/carburizing/oxidizing)
Fundamentals: no electricity needed, good for field repair and cutting

FCAW
Flux-Cored Arc Welding
Heat source: electric arc
Electrode: continuously fed tubular wire with flux core
Shielding: flux (self-shielded) or flux + external gas
Key variables: wire feed speed, voltage, polarity, gas (if used)
Fundamentals: high deposition, good for thick steel and outdoor work

UNDERWATER WELDING
Usually wet SMAW or dry hyperbaric
Heat source: electric arc
Electrode: special waterproof consumable
Shielding: flux + water/steam or pressurized habitat
Key variables: current, electrode type, depth, visibility, diver safety
Fundamentals: specialized marine/repair work, higher risk and skill required

ORBITAL WELDING
Automated circumferential welding (usually GTAW)
Heat source: electric arc
Electrode: non-consumable tungsten in rotating head
Shielding: inert gas
Key variables: rotation speed, current program, pulse settings, gas flow
Fundamentals: consistent high-quality pipe/tube welds, used in aerospace, pharma, nuclear
